Subject: generic_vhost_user: let the backend choose the device-specific features

The generic vhost-user device offers the guest only
DEFAULT_VIRTIO_FEATURES, which is the transport set (bits 28..=38).
Feature bits 0..=23 are device specific (virtio 1.4 sec 6) and never
reach the guest, so any backend whose device type has feature bits at
all is silently reduced to its featureless form.

Measured with crosvm's standalone virtio-gpu backend behind
--generic-vhost-user device_type=gpu, the guest kernel printed:

    [drm] features: -virgl -edid -resource_blob -host_visible
    [drm] features: -context_init
    [drm] number of cap sets: 0

A 2D framebuffer. VIRTIO_GPU_F_VIRGL and VIRTIO_GPU_F_CONTEXT_INIT are
what carry virgl and Venus, and both are bit 0 and bit 4.

The device cannot enumerate those bits itself -- it is generic because
it does not interpret device_type -- but it does not have to. Feature
negotiation is `avail & backend` (vu_common_ctrl.rs), so offering the
whole device-specific range delegates the choice to the only party that
knows what the bits mean: the backend. A bit the backend does not
advertise is never acked.

No effect on virtio-nvrm, by construction rather than by hope: that
backend advertises bits 28, 30 and 32 only, so the added mask ANDs to
zero and its acked_features is bit-identical. Counter-measured anyway:
gate-gpu.sh PASS and bench-transport.sh within noise.

diff --git a/virtio-devices/src/vhost_user/generic_vhost_user.rs b/virtio-devices/src/vhost_user/generic_vhost_user.rs
--- a/virtio-devices/src/vhost_user/generic_vhost_user.rs
+++ b/virtio-devices/src/vhost_user/generic_vhost_user.rs
@@ -209,7 +209,31 @@
                 | VhostUserProtocolFeatures::SHMEM
                 | VhostUserProtocolFeatures::BACKEND_REQ;
 
-            let avail_features = super::DEFAULT_VIRTIO_FEATURES;
+            // DEFAULT_VIRTIO_FEATURES carries only transport bits (28..=38).
+            // Bits 0..=23 are DEVICE SPECIFIC (virtio 1.4 sec 6), and the
+            // generic device -- which is generic precisely because it does not
+            // interpret its device_type -- cannot enumerate them.
+            //
+            // It does not have to. Negotiation is `avail & backend`
+            // (vu_common_ctrl.rs::negotiate_features_vhost_user), so offering
+            // the whole device-specific range delegates the choice to the only
+            // party that knows what the bits mean: the backend. A bit the
+            // backend does not advertise is never acked.
+            //
+            // Without this a virtio-gpu backend reaches the guest stripped:
+            // measured, the guest kernel printed
+            //     [drm] features: -virgl -edid -resource_blob -host_visible
+            //     [drm] features: -context_init
+            //     [drm] number of cap sets: 0
+            // i.e. a 2D framebuffer and no path to virgl or Venus at all.
+            //
+            // Safe for virtio-nvrm by construction, not by hope: that backend
+            // advertises 28, 30 and 32 only (crates/vhost-user-nvrm/src/nvrm.rs
+            // ::features), so the added mask ANDs to zero and acked_features is
+            // bit-identical to before.
+            const VIRTIO_DEVICE_SPECIFIC_FEATURES: u64 = (1 << 24) - 1;
+            let avail_features =
+                super::DEFAULT_VIRTIO_FEATURES | VIRTIO_DEVICE_SPECIFIC_FEATURES;
 
             let (acked_features, acked_protocol_features) =
                 vu.negotiate_features_vhost_user(avail_features, avail_protocol_features)?;
